<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dominican Order</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dominican friar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Dominican Order is a Catholic order of friars founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-dominic">Saint Dominic</a> in the early thirteenth century, dedicated to preaching and study. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a>, Dominican friars such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolome-de-las-casas">Bartolomé de las Casas</a> criticised the abuse and enslavement of indigenous Americans, influencing debates such as the Valladolid Controversy and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-laws">New Laws of 1542</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dominican-order.jpg" alt="Dominican Order" width="320" height="346" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original author unknownVectorized by ReneeWrites · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seal_of_the_Dominican_Order.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The order grew out of a medieval ideal in which men of God were no longer expected to stay behind the walls of a cloister but travelled among the people, taking the apostles of the early Church as their example. Two orders of mendicant friars came from it: the Friars Minor, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-of-assisi">Francis of Assisi</a>, and the Friars Preachers, led by the Castilian priest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-dominic">Dominic de Guzmán</a>.</p><p>In 1203 he accompanied Diego de Acebo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Osma, on a diplomatic mission to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>. The south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> was then a stronghold of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catharism">Cathars</a>, whom the Church considered heretical. In 1206 Dominic established several women he had reconciled to the faith in a convent at Prouille, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>, which became the foundation of the Dominican nuns and later his headquarters for missionary work.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Dominic set up a religious community in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a> in 1214 and in 1215 gave it an institutional form with the approval of Bishop Foulques of Toulouse. It followed the Rule of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustine-of-hippo">Saint Augustine</a>, which made its members canons regular rather than monks, able to carry out ministry and common life while living in individual poverty. Its founding documents name two purposes, preaching and the salvation of souls. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-honorius-iii">Pope Honorius III</a> approved the order by the bull Religiosam vitam on 22 December 1216.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pax-mongolica">Pax Mongolica</a> of the 13th and 14th centuries allowed missionaries to travel east. Dominican friars were preaching on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> steppes by 1225, and in 1240 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-ix">Pope Gregory IX</a> sent others to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>. Jordanus Catala de Severac went first to Persia and in 1321 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and recorded his observations in a book translated as Wonders of the East. Later, Gaspar da Cruz worked across the Portuguese empire in Asia and in 1569 or 1570 published the first European book devoted wholly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The order's highest authority is the General Chapter, made up of the Chapter of Provincials, the heads of the provinces, and the Chapter of Definitors, who represent each province. This arrangement is unique in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a>. New legislation takes effect only after three successive meetings approve it.</p><p>Friars include ordained priests and non-ordained cooperator brothers. Their life rests on four pillars: prayer, study, community and preaching, and a long period of study precedes the solemn vow. Alongside the friars stand the cloistered nuns, active sisters, whose congregations are largely a product of the 19th century, priestly fraternities, lay Dominicans and associates. Dominican friars numbered 6,058 in 2013 and 5,369 in 2024.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dominican Order” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>German merchants, many from Lübeck and Westphalia, settled below the castle, and <b>Dominican friars</b> founded the convent of St Catherine in the 1240s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/reval">Reval · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Dominican friar</b> Bartolomé de las Casas's campaign against the abuse of indigenous peoples helped produce the New Laws of 1542, which sought, with mixed success, to curb the encomienda and outlaw the enslavement of indigenous Americans, and in the Valladolid debate of 1550–1551 he argued against Juan…</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Dominican friars</b> settled in Antequera from 1528, and after the Bishopric of Oaxaca was founded in 1535 Catholic priests came in growing numbers.</blockquote><a href="/culture/zapotec-civilisation">Zapotec civilisation · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Dominican friar</b> Johann Tetzel preached it near the borders of Saxony in 1517, and people from Wittenberg crossed the border to buy.</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Dominican friar</b> Bartolomé de las Casas denounced the destruction of the Caribbean peoples in his Short Account of the Destruction of the Indies (written 1542, printed 1552), the theologian Francisco de Vitoria questioned the grounds of Spanish dominion in his lectures of 1539, and at Valladolid in…</blockquote><a href="/government/colonial-rule">Colonial rule · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Dominican friar</b> Girolamo Savonarola dominated Florence from 1494 to 1498; Anabaptists ruled Münster as a “New Jerusalem” until its prince-bishop retook the city in 1535; and historians still debate whether John Calvin’s Geneva, where a consistory of ministers and elders supervised morals from 1541, was a theocracy.</blockquote><a href="/government/theocracy">Theocracy · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
